        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.607,
            0.883,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A cuboid has the shape shown in these pictures. A useful way of thinking about a cuboid is that it is a ‘three-dimensional (3D) version’ of a rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.666,
            0.882,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It has six faces, all rectangles. It is characterised by three quantities: length, width, and height — these are denoted by l, w, h respectively."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.706,
            0.538,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You can cut out the net of a cuboid given at the end of the textbook, make appropriate folds and join the edges to form the cuboid."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.783,
            0.537,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the total surface area of a cuboid? You may refer to the net of the cuboid given in Fig. 14.2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.841,
            0.537,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Considering the faces individually, we see that the total surface area (TSA) of the cuboid is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.21,
            0.905,
            0.443,
            0.924
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_0_equation_17.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{TSA} = 2(wl + hl + hw)."
        },
